1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,987
One of the most famous cases of extinction is that of a bird known

2
00:00:06,987 --> 00:00:07,750
as the dodo.

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:13,991
In fact there's even a saying in English, 'as dead as the dodo', used

4
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,800
to refer to something which no longer exists.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:22,917
But for many centuries the dodo was alive and well, although it could only

6
00:00:22,917 --> 00:00:27,570
be found in one place, the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean.

7
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:32,040
It was a very large bird, about one metre tall,

8
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:36,190
and over the centuries it had lost the ability to fly,

9
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:40,800
but it survived happily under the trees that covered the island.

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:48,940
Then in the year 1507 the first Portuguese ships stopped at the island.

11
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:53,611
The sailors were carrying spices back to Europe,

12
00:00:53,611 --> 00:00:59,626
and found the island a convenient stopping place where they could stock up with food

13
00:00:59,626 --> 00:01:01,892
and water for the rest of the voyage,

14
00:01:01,892 --> 00:01:05,380
but they didn't settle on Mauritius.

15
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:14,660
However, in 1638 the Dutch arrived and set up a colony there.

16
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:21,325
These first human inhabitants of the island found the dodo birds a convenient source

17
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:25,260
of meat, although not everyone liked the taste.

18
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:31,760
It's hard to get an accurate description of what the dodo actually looked like.

19
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:35,256
We do have some written records from sailors,

20
00:01:35,256 --> 00:01:37,064
and a few pictures,

21
00:01:37,064 --> 00:01:40,440
but we don't know how reliable these are.

22
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:46,391
The best-known picture is a Dutch painting in which the bird appears to be

23
00:01:46,391 --> 00:01:47,874
extremely fat,

24
00:01:47,874 --> 00:01:52,944
but this may not be accurate – an Indian painting done at the same

25
00:01:52,944 --> 00:01:56,190
time shows a much thinner bird.

26
00:01:56,190 --> 00:02:03,070
Although attempts were made to preserve the bodies of some of the birds, no

27
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:06,340
complete specimen survives.

28
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:12,070
In the early 17th century four dried parts of a bird were known to

29
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:15,415
exist – of these, three have disappeared,

30
00:02:15,415 --> 00:02:24,430
so only one example of soft tissue from the dodo survives, a dodo head.

31
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:26,302
Bones have also been found,

32
00:02:26,302 --> 00:02:30,850
but there's only one complete skeleton in existence.

33
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:41,497
This single dodo skeleton has recently been the subject of scientific research which suggests

34
00:02:41,497 --> 00:02:46,890
that many of the earlier beliefs about dodos may have been incorrect.

35
00:02:46,890 --> 00:02:55,166
For example, early accounts of the birds mention how slow and clumsy it was,

36
00:02:55,166 --> 00:03:02,660
but scientists now believe the bird's strong knee joints would have made it capable

37
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:04,904
of movement which was not slow,

38
00:03:04,904 --> 00:03:07,830
but actually quite fast.

39
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:14,905
In fact, one 17th century sailor wrote that he found the birds hard to

40
00:03:14,905 --> 00:03:15,830
catch.

41
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:22,347
It's true that the dodo's small wings wouldn't have allowed it to leave the

42
00:03:22,347 --> 00:03:22,828
ground,

43
00:03:22,828 --> 00:03:30,488
but the scientists suggest that these were probably employed for balance while going over

44
00:03:30,488 --> 00:03:31,350
uneven ground.

45
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:37,470
Another group of scientists carried out analysis of the dodo's skull.

46
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:44,383
They found that the reports of the lack of intelligence of the dodo were

47
00:03:44,383 --> 00:03:46,768
not borne out by their research,

48
00:03:46,768 --> 00:03:51,356
which suggested the bird's brain was not small,

49
00:03:51,356 --> 00:03:53,400
but average in size.

50
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:59,819
In fact, in relation to its body size, it was similar to that of

51
00:03:59,819 --> 00:04:00,441
the pigeon,

52
00:04:00,441 --> 00:04:03,690
which is known to be a highly intelligent bird.

53
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:11,329
The researchers also found that the structure of the bird's skull suggested that one

54
00:04:11,329 --> 00:04:15,860
sense which was particularly well-developed was that of smell.

55
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:22,178
So the dodo may also have been particularly good at locating ripe fruit

56
00:04:22,178 --> 00:04:30,260
and other food in the island's thick vegetation.

57
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:34,796
So it looks as if the dodo was better able to survive

58
00:04:34,796 --> 00:04:38,610
and defend itself than was originally believed.

59
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:46,155
Yet less than 200 years after Europeans first arrived on the island, they had

60
00:04:46,155 --> 00:04:47,540
become extinct.

61
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,510
So what was the reason for this?

62
00:04:50,510 --> 00:04:56,224
For a long time, it was believed that the dodos were hunted to extinction,

63
00:04:56,224 --> 00:05:02,380
but scientists now believe the situation was more complicated than this.

64
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:08,823
Another factor may have been the new species brought to the island by the

65
00:05:08,823 --> 00:05:10,610
sailors.

66
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:12,034
These included dogs,

67
00:05:12,034 --> 00:05:14,640
which would have been a threat to the dodos,

68
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:16,585
and also monkeys,

69
00:05:16,585 --> 00:05:22,500
which ate the fruit that was the main part of the dodos' diet.

70
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,328
These were brought to the island deliberately,

71
00:05:25,328 --> 00:05:29,621
but the ships also brought another type of creature – rats,

72
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:36,100
which came to land from the ships and rapidly overran the island.

73
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:42,211
These upset the ecology of the island, not just the dodos

74
00:05:42,211 --> 00:05:43,880
but other species too.

75
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:52,274
However, they were a particular danger to the dodos because they consumed their eggs,

76
00:05:52,274 --> 00:05:59,086
and since each dodo only laid one at a time, this probably had a

77
00:05:59,086 --> 00:06:01,470
devastating effect on populations.

78
00:06:01,470 --> 00:06:10,014
However, we now think that probably the main cause of the birds' extinction was

79
00:06:10,014 --> 00:06:13,183
not the introduction of non-native species,

80
00:06:13,183 --> 00:06:15,870
but the introduction of agriculture.

81
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:22,463
This meant that the forest that had once covered all the island,

82
00:06:22,463 --> 00:06:27,955
and that had provided a perfect home for the dodo, was cut down

83
00:06:27,955 --> 00:06:30,600
so that crops such as sugar could be grown.

84
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:41,000
So although the dodo had survived for thousands of years, suddenly it was gone.
